### What is the problem?

Given a string, count how many contiguous substrings are palindromes, counting each position separately even if the text is identical. For "aaa", the answer is 6: three singles, two pairs, and one triple. This is the counting companion to the longest palindromic substring.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class PalindromicSubstringsDP {
    public static int countSubstrings(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n][n];
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            dp[i][i] = true;
            count++;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j) && (j - i < 2 || dp[i + 1][j - 1])) {
                    dp[i][j] = true;
                    count++;
                }
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

### Why does this matter in banking?

The security team at the National Bank of India computes a symmetry score for batch file identifiers. A high palindrome count relative to the string length signals a highly structured, likely machine-generated identifier. Rajesh flags identifiers where this ratio exceeds a threshold for manual review.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dsa.PalindromicSubstringsDP;

public class SymmetryScorer {
    public void score(String id) {
        int count = PalindromicSubstringsDP.countSubstrings(id);
        System.out.println("ID: " + id + ", Palindrome count: " + count);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new SymmetryScorer().score("ABCBA");
        new SymmetryScorer().score("AAAA");
        new SymmetryScorer().score("ABCD");
    }
}
```

```text
ID: ABCBA, Palindrome count: 7
ID: AAAA, Palindrome count: 10
ID: ABCD, Palindrome count: 4
```

### How does the DP counting work?

Reuse the same boolean palindrome table from the longest palindromic substring. Each time `dp[i][j]` is set to true, increment the counter. The recurrence is identical: a range is a palindrome when the end characters match and either the range is short or the inner range is already a palindrome.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class CountingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "ABA";
        int n = s.length();
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n][n];
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            dp[i][i] = true;
            count++;
            System.out.println("Palindrome [" + i + "," + i + "]: " + s.charAt(i) + " count=" + count);
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j) && (j - i < 2 || dp[i + 1][j - 1])) {
                    dp[i][j] = true;
                    count++;
                    System.out.println("Palindrome [" + i + "," + j + "]: " + s.substring(i, j + 1) + " count=" + count);
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Total: " + count);
    }
}
```

```text
Palindrome [2,2]: A count=1
Palindrome [1,1]: B count=2
Palindrome [0,0]: A count=3
Palindrome [0,2]: ABA count=4
Total: 4
```

### How does this compare to the expand-around-center approach?

The expand-around-center approach (Q1448) counts palindromes in O(n squared) time with O(1) space, while the DP approach uses O(n squared) space. The DP table is useful when other queries need to check arbitrary ranges. For standalone counting, expand-around-center is more space-efficient.

