### What are the edge cases?

A single character has exactly 1 palindrome. An empty string has 0. Two identical characters have 3 (two singles plus one pair). All distinct characters have exactly n palindromes (only the singles).

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(PalindromicSubstringsDP.countSubstrings("A"));    // 1
        System.out.println(PalindromicSubstringsDP.countSubstrings(""));     // 0
        System.out.println(PalindromicSubstringsDP.countSubstrings("AA"));   // 3
        System.out.println(PalindromicSubstringsDP.countSubstrings("AB"));   // 2
    }
}
```

```text
1
0
3
2
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(n squared) because each cell in the n-by-n table takes O(1) to compute. Space is O(n squared) for the boolean table. Manacher's algorithm can compute palindrome radii in O(n) and sum them for O(n) total counting.

### What are common mistakes?

Forgetting to count single-character palindromes as base cases. Using an integer DP table (for subsequence length) instead of a boolean table (for substring palindrome check). Confusing the substring count with the subsequence count.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you count distinct palindromic substrings?
- How would you count palindromic subsequences instead of substrings?
- How would you use this palindrome table for the minimum-cut partitioning problem?
- What is the relationship between the palindrome count and string complexity?
